"""Refresh P10 reflection only after all recorded P9 statuses are resolved."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
import re
from html import escape

out=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
data=json.loads((out/'resolved_tickets.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
assert data['complete'], 'Do not describe unfinished runs as complete.'
tickets={t['ticket_id']:t for t in data['tickets']}
def roles(tid): return escape(', '.join(tickets[tid]['agents_participated']))
def tools(tid): return escape(', '.join(dict.fromkeys(x['tool'] for x in tickets[tid]['mcp_tools_used'])))
reflection=f'''<section id="reflection" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-reflection" tabindex="0" hidden><div class="coming">
<h2>Reflection</h2>
<p class="source">Final evidence: <a href="resolved_tickets.json">resolved_tickets.json</a>, <a href="audit_trail.json">audit_trail.json</a>, Actual sections, Cash, and <a href="resolved_board.html">resolved dashboard screenshots</a>. All three stored statuses are resolved, but their outcomes differ: Ticket 101 was fulfilled in a disclosed simulation, Ticket 102 was paid, and Ticket 103 was declined. Earlier failed and awaiting-human runs remain in the audit.</p>
<h3>1. Ticket performance and Expected versus Actual</h3>
<h4>Ticket 101 — accurate stock reasoning; expensive recovery before simulated fulfillment</h4>
<p>Participants: {roles(101)}. The initial Boss → Inventory → Accounting chain matched Expected. Inventory correctly found zero small white tees, and Accounting created invoice request #1 for $840 while reserving awareness of rent. Inventory saved one tee purchase draft. However, callbacks and repeated reads exhausted the initial 24-request cap; provider token-count and Windows audit-write problems also interrupted earlier attempts. Customer Service was missing from that first preparation.</p>
<p>After the human approved payment and explicitly permitted made-up supply facts provided they were called out, the continuation verified paid invoice 501, invoice coverage with no duplicate charge, and one recorded small tee. Inventory and Customer Service explicitly identified the quote and receipt as <strong>user-authorized simulation</strong>. Customer Service saved an unsent pre-fulfillment update before Boss used fulfill_ticket_order, taking that recorded stock from one to zero and resolving the ticket. The final report then exceeded an 8,000-input-token cap. A separate dashboard read-only review completed the Boss report without repeating fulfillment. The input cap was raised to 12,000 for subsequent runs and then to 16,000 after the final requirements review, with the shared 80,000-token, 40-request, $1 estimated-cost and timeout guards retained.</p>
<p>This followed the Expected stock/invoice/human checkpoints and eventually used the planned Customer Service role. The key departure is the authorized simulation: the $8 quote, paid-invoice coverage, and receipt were not obtained from a supplier or physically observed. The assignment demonstrates the guarded workflow; it does not establish a real tee delivery. Actual tools across attempts: {tools(101)}.</p>
<h4>Ticket 102 — the cleanest division of responsibility</h4>
<p>Participants: {roles(102)}. Boss contacted Facilities, which delegated financial verification to Accounting, as Expected. The team used the August 31, 2026 shop date to identify September 2 rent as upcoming, not overdue. Accounting initially prepared request #2 for $2,400. After named human approval posted the payment, the continuation checked its paid record and the advanced lease due date, then Boss used close_paid_rent_ticket. Inventory and Customer Service stayed out.</p>
<p>Actual reports returned through the requesting agent rather than requiring an extra Accounting → Boss callback. The payment was executed by the human API route, superseding the older Expected executor sequence. Closing the notice required paid evidence, not merely a request or an agent's assurance. This was a genuine database payment/closure outcome within the simulated shop. Actual tools: {tools(102)}.</p>
<h4>Ticket 103 — a useful feasibility check; resolution by decline</h4>
<p>Participants: {roles(103)}. Boss → Inventory → Accounting matched the revised Expected Inventory-first plan. The team correctly identified 8 medium hoodies against 20 requested: 12 missing, costing $264 at the $22 stored planning cost. After invoice and rent, checking held $160, leaving a $104 planning-cost deficit. This was not a confirmed supplier quote; the vendor catalog also did not prove hoodie availability.</p>
<p>The first run stored a proposed $52 unit price but saved the customer draft before recording that proposal. The draft therefore did not convey the later offer—an ordering miss against Expected. The final continuation followed the human's explicit direction to decline. Customer Service saved a new unsent decline explanation, and Boss used the new decline_ticket_request tool. The record says declined, so resolved must not be read as twenty hoodies fulfilled. No hoodie purchase, receipt, inventory deduction, or cash outflow occurred; the old $52 proposal remains historical and is superseded.</p>
<p>Expected anticipated a conditional restock/discount path and an open ticket while funds or stock were absent. Actual instead reached a final shop decision using a new guarded tool. That extension was needed to distinguish a completed refusal from an unfulfilled order. Facilities remained unnecessary. Actual tools: {tools(103)}.</p>
<h3>2. Cash and human control</h3>
<p>Checking started at <strong>$3,400.00</strong>. Ticket 101's invoice payment reduced it by <strong>$840.00</strong>, to $2,560; its simulated tee was covered by that invoice, so there was no extra $8 purchase charge. Ticket 102 rent reduced it by <strong>$2,400.00</strong>, to <strong>$160.00</strong>. Ticket 103 changed cash by <strong>$0.00</strong>. $3,400 − $840 − $2,400 = $160, exactly matching cash_accounts and the payment ledger. Both payments record Roman's explicit approval. No real bank transfer or incoming revenue was modeled.</p>
<h3>3. What would have been simpler with one agent using tools?</h3>
<p>One agent could verify the rent context, prepare one request, stop for approval, and close after re-reading the paid record. Repeating the same evidence across Facilities, Accounting, and Boss added context overhead. For the tee, one agent could inspect exact-size stock and the linked invoice, account for shared rent, then prepare the missing records without recursive callbacks. For the hoodie request, one agent could compute the shortage/funding gap and store the final decision before drafting its explanation, avoiding the price/draft mismatch.</p>
<p>The dashboard's specialist contributions made responsibilities legible, and Inventory-first prevented a price-only answer to an infeasible order. Nonetheless, full connectivity caused avoidable requests and large final contexts. A single agent would simplify orchestration, not remove human approval, physical-evidence requirements, or the $104 deficit. Ticket 101's simulation was a human-authorized workaround, not an agent discovery.</p>
<h3>4. Three new problems supported by the current tools</h3>
<p>These are hypothetical future cases, not additional completed runs. They assume a valid ticket is stored externally. New-ticket intake is absent, and the current team runner selects 101–103; new IDs would require extending that runner's selection rule. The underlying MCP operations can handle the following same-schema cases.</p>
<ol><li><strong>An order for two blue Yale caps.</strong> With 40 size-OS caps currently recorded, Inventory can use read_shop_board to establish exact stock and Boss can fulfill_ticket_order for a valid customer-order record. Customer Service can save an unsent update before closure. No modeled revenue or restock is needed.</li>
<li><strong>A future notice for the stored lease with sufficient cash.</strong> Facilities can inspect its date/amount, Accounting can request_payment, and the human can approve_and_pay. Boss can close_paid_rent_ticket only after paid evidence. The current $160 cannot fund another $2,400 payment, so this example expressly depends on sufficient permitted starting funds.</li>
<li><strong>A four-medium-hoodie discount request.</strong> The eight recorded medium units can support four. Inventory checks feasibility, Accounting checks cost/margin, Boss records a justified discount decision, and Customer Service drafts it before guarded fulfillment. Alternatively, an infeasible price request can be declined with a saved explanation. No customer acceptance should be invented.</li></ol>
<h3>5. Three problems requiring additional capabilities</h3>
<ol><li><strong>A damaged-item return and refund.</strong> Existing payment kinds cover invoices, rent, and purchases, not refunds. Customer Service, Inventory, and Accounting would need a sales/returns ledger, inspection and inventory-disposition tools, and a separately human-approved refund operation. A new agent alone would not provide those records.</li>
<li><strong>Verifying a hoodie supplier's actual quote and shipment.</strong> A Procurement role would need a verified SKU/supplier catalog, quote retrieval, and shipment-status tools with provenance. Real vendor contact is prohibited in this assignment; a permitted simulated source or changed scope would be needed. The authorized tee simulation is not a substitute for these integrations or physical receipt evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Repairing a leaking pipe in the shop.</strong> Facilities currently has lease/rent evidence, not work orders, contractors, estimates, or repair completion records. It would need maintenance and contractor tools plus human-confirmed completion; Accounting would need a supported repair obligation and guarded human payment approval.</li></ol>
<p class="finish"><strong>Assessment:</strong> All three requests now have final stored decisions, with cash protected and exceptions disclosed. The strongest results were exact-size feasibility and payment guards; the weakest were redundant callbacks, large contexts, and draft sequencing. Historical failures remain visible rather than being rewritten as successful runs.</p>
</div></section>'''
page=out/'desk_tickets.html'
source=page.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
expected=re.findall(r'<section class="expected".*?</section>',source,re.S)
source=re.sub(r'<section id="reflection".*?</section>',lambda _:reflection,source,flags=re.S)
assert expected==re.findall(r'<section class="expected".*?</section>',source,re.S)
page.write_text(source,encoding='utf-8')
(out/'p10_final_reflection.html').write_text(reflection,encoding='utf-8')
print('Reflection refreshed from final stored outcomes; prior reflection fragment preserved separately.')
